  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/smaragdina-reg",
      "html": "<ul><li>IUCN Red List: Data Deficient.</li><li>Part of the Betta splendens complex.</li><li>Natural wildtype colouration: metallic green with blue iridescence.</li><li>Can be identified by the snakescale-like pattern on the face.</li><li>Hardy and good for beginners, as it can tolerate low temperatures.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/smaragdina-reg",
      "html": "<p>Be sure to have the habitat ready before purchase. The fish offered are quality Betta smaragdina bred by a breeder in Vietnam.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/smaragdina-reg",
      "html": "<p>Native to northeastern Thailand, Laos, and parts of Cambodia in the Mekong Basin. These fish originate from the Khorat Plateau in Thailand, a harsher environment than that of other splendens.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/smaragdina-reg",
      "html": "<p>Inhabits slow-moving, shallow waters such as rice paddies, swamps, ponds, and streams with dense vegetation and leaf litter. Thrives in warm (24–30°C), slightly acidic to neutral (pH 5.5–7.5) water with low oxygen, relying on its labyrinth organ for air-breathing.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/smaragdina-reg",
      "html": "<ul><li>Temperature: 24–29°C (75–85°F); much better colouration at a warmer temperature above 26°C (78°F). Can tolerate 16°C (60°F) for a short period.</li><li>pH: 5.0–7.0</li><li>TDS: 60–200. Kept in soft water with low TDS to match other wild bettas, but more tolerant of water conditions.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/smaragdina-reg",
      "html": "<p>Carnivorous. Fed live blackworms, frozen bloodworms, and pellets.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/smaragdina-reg",
      "html": "<p>Males are VERY aggressive and should be kept alone or in tanks with non-male betta mates. Can be kept in a planted community tank of multiple females if there is adequate space.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/smaragdina-reg",
      "html": "<p>Body: slender and elongated, slightly compressed, reaching a maximum standard length of about 7 cm. Tail: rounded or slightly crescent-shaped caudal fin, shorter and less ornate than Betta splendens, often with a red distal band with faint black edging or transverse bars.</p><p>Males: dark brown to black body with iridescent green or blue scales giving a metallic \"emerald\" sheen; vivid red accents on caudal, anal, and pelvic fins during courtship or aggression. The operculum has iridescent green or blue scales with two parallel red or silvery vertical bars, more vibrant when flaring.</p><p>Females: duller, brownish with muted green or red hints and reduced iridescence; simpler, less distinct opercular markings with faint iridescence.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/smaragdina-reg",
      "html": "<p>Bubblenester.</p>"
    }
  ],
